Then buildTerrain(scene), buildWater(scene, heightTex), buildSky(scene), buildVegetation(scene), buildLandmarks(scene), buildCampfire.

Actually fewer files: world.js exports everything. main.js orchestrates.

Terrain detail texture canvas: 256 noise grayscale.

Tree merged geometry helper mergeGeoms(geoms): all non-indexed, concat position/normal/uv. 

```js
function mergeGeometries(geoms) {
  const nonIndexed = geoms.map(g => g.index ? g.toNonIndexed() : g);
  let total = 0;
  nonIndexed.forEach(g => total += g.attributes.position.count);
  const pos = new Float32Array(total*3), nor = new Float32Array(total*3), uv = new Float32Array(total*2);
  let o3=0, o2=0;
  for (const g of nonIndexed) {
    pos.set(g.attributes.position.array, o3);
    nor.set(g.attributes.normal.array, o3);
    if (g.attributes.uv) uv.set(g.attributes.uv.array, o2);
    o3 += g.attributes.position.count*3; o2 += g.attributes.position.count*2;
  }
  const out = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
  out.setAttribute('position', new THREE.BufferAttribute(pos,3));
  out.setAttribute('normal', new THREE.BufferAttribute(nor,3));
  out.setAttribute('uv', new THREE.BufferAttribute(uv,2));
  return out;
}
```

Terrain height function final:

```js
export function terrainHeight(x, z) {
  const hills = fbm2(x * 0.011 + 3.1, z * 0.011 + 7.7, 5) * 12.5
              + fbm2(x * 0.047 + 13.2, z * 0.047 + 5.9, 3) * 3.0;

  // Northern granite massif
  const northMask = smoothstep(50, -135, z);
  let r = ridged2(x * 0.0075 + 11.7, z * 0.0075 + 5.1, 5);
  r = Math.pow(r, 2.3) * 125;
  const mountains = r * northMask;

  // Enclosing rim (east / west / south)
  const rimX = smoothstep(145, 210, Math.abs(x)) * (26 + 20 * fbm2(x * 0.02 + 5, z * 0.02 + 1, 3));
  const rimZ = smoothstep(165, 212, z) * (22 + 18 * fbm2(x * 0.02 + 9, z * 0.02 + 4, 3));

  // Crystal Lake basin
  const dL2 = dist2(x, z, 0, 34);
  const lake = -21 * Math.exp(-dL2 / (40 * 40));

  // Rock shelf for the Great Falls (north shore of the lake)
  const dF2 = dist2(x, z, 10, -14);
  const fallsShelf = 10.5 * Math.exp(-dF2 / (8.5 * 8.5));

  return hills + mountains + rimX + rimZ + lake + fallsShelf + 5.5;
}
```

Check falls location: at (10, -14): hills ~ ±12, northMask at z=-14 → smoothstep(50,-135,-14): t=(−14−50)/(−135−50)=(−64)/(−185)=0.346 → smooth 0.346²(3−2*0.346)=0.1197*(2.308)=0.276. Hmm mountains add r²·125·0.276. If r≈0.5→0.25·125·0.276≈8.6; r≈0.6→0.36·125·0.276≈12.4. Plus fallsShelf 10.5, +5.5, hills ±12, lake: d² from (10,-14) to (0,34) = 100+2304=2404, /1600=1.5, exp(-1.5)=0.22 → -4.7. Total ≈ 0.5·... let me estimate typical: hills 0 + mountains 10 + shelf 10.5 − lake 4.7 + 5.5 ≈ 21. Hmm that's higher than water, falls from 21 looks tall but ok? Water plane from ~10? I want shelf ~9-10 above water. Reduce shelf to... Actually let me just sample terrainHeight at runtime in JS and position the waterfall plane from (shelfY) down to 0 automatically. That's robust regardless of exact math. Similarly POI positions computed at runtime via sampling. 

